<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="ws_3_4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>3.4 Practice Worksheet</title>


<introduction>
    <p>
    <url href="https://youtu.be/FWjLRIKyF-g">Click here for a walkthrough video of some problem below.</url>
    </p>
</introduction>


<exercisegroup>
<title>Solving Quadratic Equations</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Use an appropriate method to solve each quadratic equation.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>(x+1)(x-5)=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Because this is already factored, set each factor to <m>0</m>. Therefore <m>x+1=0</m> and <m>x-5=0</m>, implying <m>x=-1</m> and <m>x=5</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>(4k+5)(k-1)=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Because this is already factored, set each factor to <m>0</m>. Therefore <m>4k+5=0</m> and <m>k-1=0</m>, implying <m>k=-\frac{5}{4}</m> and <m>k=1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>x^2-13x-14=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This can be factored to <m>(x-14)(x+1)=0</m>. Therefore, <m>x=14</m> and <m>x=-1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>4n^2-25=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This is a difference of squares: <m>(2n)^2-5^2=0</m>. Therefore it can be factored to <m>(2n+5)(2n-5)=0</m>. Of course, you could use the <m>ac</m>-method in section 3.2 as well. Now, <m>2n+5=0</m> so <m>n=-\frac{5}{2}</m> and <m>2n-5=0</m> so <m>n=\frac{5}{2}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>3x^2+5x-2=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here we'll use the quadratic formula:
</p>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>x \amp \quad = \quad \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-4ac} }{2a}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \frac{-5 \pm \sqrt{5^2-4(3)(-2)} }{2(3)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \frac{-5 \pm \sqrt{25+24} }{6}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \frac{-5 \pm \sqrt{49} }{6}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \frac{-5 \pm 7}{6}</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Now, <m>\frac{-5+7}{6} = \frac{2}{6}=\frac{1}{3}</m> and <m>\frac{-5-7}{6} = \frac{-12}{6}=-2</m> so the solutions are <m>x=\frac{1}{3}</m> and <m>x=-2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>x^2+8x=-15</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First, rewrite the equation as <m>x^2+8x+15=0</m>. This factors to <m>(x+5)(x+3)=0</m>. Therefore, <m>x+5=0</m> and <m>x+3=0</m>, implying the solutions are <m>x=-5</m> and <m>x=-3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>x(x-20)=-100</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We must first distribute the <m>x</m> to get <m>x^2 - 20x = -100</m> and then add <m>100</m> to both sides:
<md>
x^2-20x+100=0.
</md> Now, we can factor this equation to <m>(x-10)(x-10)=0</m>, implying the solution is <m>x=10</m>.

<em>If you did not do this first step, you might be saying that from <m>x(x-20)=-100</m> it follows that <m>x=-100</m> and <m>x-20=-100</m>. This is NOT true! Remember the zero-product rule only works if the equation is set to <m>0</m>, and not some other number like <m>-100</m>.</em>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>9y^2-11=6y</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Subtract <m>6y</m> from both sides to obtain <m>9y^2-6y-11=0</m>. Using the quadratic formula, then
</p>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>y \amp \quad = \quad \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-4ac} }{2a}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \frac{6 \pm \sqrt{(-6)^2-4(9)(-11)} }{2(9)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \frac{6\pm \sqrt{36+396} }{18}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \frac{6 \pm \sqrt{432} }{18} \qquad \text{(can leave it like this)}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

</solution>
</exercise>


</exercisegroup>


<exercisegroup>
<title>Computing the Discriminant</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Compute the discriminant of each quadratic equation. What does it tell you about the number of solutions that the equation will have?
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>6p^2-2p-3=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here <m>a=6</m>, <m>b=-2</m>, and <m>c=-3</m> so the discriminant is
<md>
D \quad = \quad b^2-4ac \quad = \quad (-2)^2-4(6)(-3) \quad = \quad 76.
</md> Because <m>D \gt 0</m>, there are two distinct real solutions.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>9n^2-3n+2=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here <m>a=9</m>, <m>b=-3</m>, and <m>c=2</m> so the discriminant is
<md>
D \quad = \quad b^2-4ac \quad = \quad (-3)^2-4(9)(2) \quad = \quad -63.
</md> Because <m>D \lt 0</m>, there are no real solutions.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>x^2-2x-15=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here <m>a=1</m>, <m>b=-2</m>, and <m>c=-15</m> so the discriminant is
<md>
D \quad = \quad b^2-4ac \quad = \quad (-2)^2-4(1)(-15) \quad = \quad 64.
</md> Because <m>D \gt 0</m>, there are two distinct real solutions.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>-x^2-9=6x</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Rewrite this as <m>-x^2-6x-9=0</m> by subtracting <m>6x</m> from both sides. Then, <m>a=-1</m>, <m>b=-6</m>, and <m>c=-9</m> so the discriminant is
<md>
D \quad = \quad b^2-4ac \quad = \quad (-6)^2-4(-1)(-9) \quad = \quad 0.
</md> Because <m>D=0</m>, there is one real solution.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>-2x^2-8x-14=-6</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Rewrite this as <m>-2x^2-8x-8=0</m>. Then <m>a=-2</m>, <m>b=-8</m>, and <m>c=-8</m> so the discriminant is
<md>
D \quad = \quad b^2-4ac \quad = \quad (-8)^2-4(-2)(-8) \quad = \quad 0.
</md> Because <m>D=0</m>, there is one real solution.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>n(8-7n)=0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Rewrite this as <m>8n-7n^2=0</m>, or <m>-7n^2+8n=0</m>. Then <m>a=-7</m>, <m>b=8</m>, and <m>c=0</m> so the discriminant is
<md>
D \quad = \quad b^2-4ac \quad = \quad 8^2-4(-7)(0) \quad = \quad 64.
</md> Because <m>D\gt 0</m>, there are two distinct real solutions.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</exercisegroup>




<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find the formula for the quadratic function whose graph has <m>x</m>-intercepts <m>(4,0)</m> and <m>(10,0)</m> and <m>y</m>-intercept <m>(0,20)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Given the <m>x</m>-intercepts <m>x_1=4</m> and <m>x_2=10</m>, then using <em>factored form</em> we have
<md>
y \quad = \quad a(x-x_1)(x-x_2)  \quad = \quad a(x-4)(x-10).
</md> We need the other point <m>(0,20)</m> to determine <m>a</m>. Plugging this in,
<md>
20 \quad = \quad a(0-4)(0-10).
</md> Therefore, <m>20=a(-4)(-10)</m> so <m>20=a(40)</m> and thus <m>a=\frac{1}{2}</m>. So
<md>
y = \frac{1}{2}(x-4)(x-10).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find the formula for the quadratic function whose graph has vertex <m>(-3,8)</m> and <m>y</m>-intercept <m>(0,-5)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Given the vertex <m>h=-3</m> and <m>k=8</m>, then
<md>
y \quad = \quad a(x-h)^2+k \quad = \quad a(x+3)^2+8.
</md> We use the other point <m>(0,-5)</m> to solve for <m>a</m>. Plugging this in above,
<md>
-5 \quad = \quad a(0+3)^2+8.
</md> This means that <m>-5 = a(9)+8</m> so <m>-13=9a</m> implying that <m>a=\frac{-13}{9} </m>. So
<md>
y = -\frac{13}{9} (x+3)^2+8.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find the equation in factored form for the graphed quadratic function.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_3_4_1">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_3_4_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-6,xmax=4,
ymin=-4,ymax=13,
xtick={-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4},
ytick={-4,-2,...,12},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-6:4 ,thick] {-0.75*(x+1)^2+12};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
One approach is to use the two horizontal intercepts <m>x_1=-5</m> and <m>x_2=3</m>. Then
<md>
y \quad = \quad a(x-x_1)(x-x_2) \quad = \quad a(x+5)(x-3).
</md> To determine <m>a</m>, we need to use another point on the graph, say <m>(-1,12)</m>. Plugging this in,
<md>
12 \quad = \quad a(-1+5)(-1-3) \quad = \quad a(4)(-4).
</md> Thus <m>12=-16a</m> and so <m>a=\frac{-12}{16} = \frac{-3}{4} </m>. This means
<md>
y = \frac{-3}{4} (x+5)(x-3).
</md> <em>Note that using the vertex <m>(-1,12)</m>, like the next question, will give you an equation for the quadratic function but it will not be in <alert>factored form</alert> as this problem is requiring.</em>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Find the equation in vertex form for the graphed quadratic function.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_3_4_2">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_3_4_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-3,xmax=7,
ymin=-13,ymax=3,
xtick={-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7},
ytick={-12,-10,...,2},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-3:7 ,thick] {0.75*(x-2)^2-12};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We use the vertex <m>(2,-12)</m> here to get
<md>
y \quad = \quad a(x-2)^2-12.
</md> To determine <m>a</m>, use another point on the graph like <m>(6,0)</m> for example. Plugging this in,
<md>
0 \quad = \quad a(6-2)^2-12.
</md> Therefore <m>0=16a-12</m> and so <m>a=\frac{3}{4}</m>. This implies
<md>
y = \frac{3}{4}(x-2)^2-12.
</md> <em>Note that using the two horizontal intercepts, like the previous question, will give you an equation for the quadratic function but it will not be in <alert>vertex form</alert> as this problem is requiring.</em>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</worksheet>